 * /services/geo-methodology/ — the anti-snake-oil page for the GEO market.
 * Linked from the services index, the geo-audit rung's notIncluded line, and
 * the geo-audit page's own "why trust this audit" callout — this is where
 * "nobody can honestly promise rankings" gets spelled out instead of just
 * asserted. It states plainly what AI-visibility measurement CAN show, what
 * it CANNOT, and how the €900 audit samples — numbers here must always match
 * what that page actually delivers (see src/data/services.ts, geo-audit rung).
 *
 * Structure: every h2 is a buyer question in disguise, restated as a literal
 * question so the visible copy and the FAQPage node below can share text
 * verbatim (Google requires the match; a mismatch is a manual-action risk).
 * Each answer is stored as an ARRAY of paragraphs (`body: string[]`) and the
 * FAQPage answer is `body.join(' ')` — the rendered text and the JSON-LD are
 * therefore the same characters by construction, while the reader gets
 * paragraphs instead of a 780-character wall. Never render `body[0]` alone,
 * and never let a heading or an eyebrow into the answer string.
 * The closing callout is deliberately OUTSIDE the FAQ loop — it carries the
 * two links (to the audit and to Trust & Process) that back the standing
 * house rule: read this before buying an audit from anyone, including this
 * studio.
 *
 * MEASUREMENT-HONESTY NOTE (2026-08-24, and the reason section 02 reads the
 * way it does). This page used to list `llms.txt` and structured data among
 * "the technical inputs that make citation possible", and the €900 audit is
 * sold partly on that line. That claim is not supportable:
 *   - Google's own AI-optimization guide
 *     (developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide,
 *     read 2026-08-24) states "You don't need to create new machine readable
 *     files, AI text files, markup, or Markdown to appear in Google Search"
 *     and that such files "will neither harm nor help your site's visibility
 *     or rankings in Google Search, as Google Search ignores them";
 *   - the same guide states "Structured data isn't required for generative
 *     AI search, and there's no special schema.org markup you need to add"
 *     — it stays recommended as ordinary SEO, not as an AI-citation lever;
 *   - Gary Illyes said at Search Central Live (July 2025) that Google does
 *     not support llms.txt and is not planning to;
 *   - server-log studies published through 2026 report AI crawlers almost
 *     never requesting /llms.txt.
 * So the file is now described as something the audit RECORDS, not something
 * it credits. The same overclaim still stands elsewhere and was NOT edited
 * here (those files are owned by other work in flight on this branch) —
 * grep `llms.txt` in src/data/services.ts (the geo-audit deliverable, the
 * retainer's monthly fixes, the rung's SEO description, the web-build rung),
 * src/data/services-faq.ts (the "does GEO replace SEO" answer),
 * src/pages/[lang]/services/geo-audit/index.astro and public/llms.txt.
 * Reported to the owner rather than changed under someone else's hands.
 *
 * Standing content laws enforced elsewhere on the site: no fabricated
 * rankings, no round-the-clock SLA promise, no payment provider we cannot
 * actually use — see tests/unit/content-laws.test.ts.
